0.0270538330078125, -0.0240325927734375, 0.07135009765625, -0.0077362060546875, 0.037017822265625, -0.04534912109375, -0.00701141357421875, -0.0261688232421875, 0.0022373199462890625, 0.00988006591796875, -0.01837158203125, -0.008636474609375, -0.0498046875, 0.0014162063598632812, -0.0061798095703125, 0.006664276123046875, 0.0011148452758789062, -0.039093017578125, 0.003597259521484375, -0.0021076202392578125, -0.019622802734375, -0.0162353515625, 0.0089569091796875, -0.005832672119140625, 0.0017261505126953125, -0.00412750244140625, -0.006694793701171875, -0.003040313720703125, 0.01030731201171875, -0.01227569580078125, -0.0207672119140625, 0.031219482421875, 0.08349609375, 0.004863739013671875, 0.011962890625, -0.0032863616943359375, -0.03265380859375, 0.0307159423828125, 0.006866455078125, 0.0095062255859375, -0.0146026611328125, 0.03204345703125, 0.00966644287109375, -0.01535797119140625, 0.00689697265625, 0.00400543212890625, 0.01108551025390625, -0.0037708282470703125, 0.01348114013671875, 0.004817962646484375, -0.006511688232421875, 0.0071258544921875, 0.004627227783203125, -0.003795623779296875, 0.006374359130859375, 0.011444091796875, -0.004276275634765625, -0.01947021484375, -0.0063323974609375, 0.0162200927734375, -0.01340484619140625, 0.038818359375, 0.0240020751953125, -0.0174407958984375, 0.021514892578125, -0.009368896484375, -0.0127105712890625, -0.0025577545166015625, 0.0279388427734375, -0.01751708984375, -0.01214599609375, 0.024200439453125, 0.0260009765625, 0.0012216567993164062, 0.004886627197265625, -0.002105712890625, 0.00888824462890625, -0.023223876953125, 0.021240234375, 0.01045989990234375, 0.01079559326171875, 0.07562255859375, -0.0031909942626953125, -0.0067596435546875, -0.001983642578125, 0.04595947265625, 0.0032939910888671875, 0.0229339599609375, 0.0161285400390625, 0.045806884765625, -0.0077667236328125, 0.007328033447265625, -0.014251708984375, 0.0251312255859375, 0.0030040740966796875, 0.033355712890625, -0.01329803466796875, -0.01267242431640625, -0.010955810546875, 0.0396728515625, 0.0023517608642578125, -0.00299072265625, 0.039398193359375, -0.0178070068359375, 0.01483154296875, 0.003002166748046875, 0.01629638671875, 0.0310211181640625, 0.01885986328125, 0.03619384765625, -0.014007568359375, 0.03338623046875, 0.0159149169921875, 0.0119781494140625, -0.01309967041015625, -0.005641937255859375, -0.003452301025390625, -0.0298614501953125, 0.00551605224609375, -0.006725311279296875, -0.0162200927734375, 0.01354217529296875, 0.057952880859375, -0.027130126953125, -0.0062408447265625, -0.00955963134765625, 0.0169525146484375, -0.00348663330078125, -0.0100250244140625, 0.025054931640625, 0.01953125, -0.0035343170166015625, -0.011016845703125, -0.01348876953125, -0.00902557373046875, 0.0039520263671875, -0.025848388671875, 0.006549835205078125, -0.0292816162109375, -0.0119781494140625, -0.0019502639770507812, -0.01043701171875, 0.0152435302734375, 0.0112152099609375, 0.0079498291015625, -0.0195465087890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. In this context:\n\n- **Capability:** A distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text prompts), image editing (modifying images based on instructions), or associating images with external knowledge (linking images to structured entities).\n- **Capability dependencies:** Relationships indicating that mastering one capability (e.g., text-to-image) facilitates learning another (e.g., image editing).\n- **Capability milestones:** Predefined performance thresholds (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) that indicate when a model has sufficiently acquired a capability to progress to more advanced tasks.\n- **Caption experts:** Ensembles of automated and human-in-the-loop systems that generate and harmonize image descriptions and entity tags across tasks, ensuring consistency and semantic alignment.\n- **Harmonization module:** A system component that standardizes and aligns captions and entity tags across different data sources and tasks, producing a unified, consistent dataset for training.\n- **Curriculum scheduling:** Sequencing training data so that the model learns foundational capabilities before progressing to more complex ones.\n\n**Workflow Overview**\n\nThe workflow proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **Data engines** generate and curate supervision for each capability:\n   - **Grounding Engine:** Constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation using automated web-scale scraping, filtering, and caption generation with large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for descriptive accuracy.\n   - **Transformation Engine:** Creates inter-image editing pairs by applying controlled transformations (e.g., object insertion, removal, style transfer) using automated image editing pipelines, labeling before/after pairs with specific edit descriptions.\n   - **Association Engine:** Links images to structured knowledge entities by matching image content to entries in external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata) using automated entity recognition models.\n\n2. **Caption experts** harmonize descriptions and entity tags across all data, ensuring consistency in descriptions and facilitating cross-task learning. Human annotators review high-value or ambiguous cases.\n\n3. The **harmonization module** integrates these outputs, standardizing terminology and annotation formats to produce a unified dataset.\n\n4. The **curriculum scheduler** sequences data exposure based on capability dependencies and model progress, dynamically adjusting the mix of data as the model reaches capability milestones.\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Capability Dependencies**\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on empirically established capability dependencies. To determine these dependencies, we conduct transfer experiments as follows:\n\n- **Experimental Design:** For each pair of capabilities, we pretrain small models (n=3 per experiment, each with 500k parameters) on one capability (e.g., T2I) and then fine-tune on another (e.g., editing) using a fixed dataset of 100k samples per capability. Tasks and datasets are selected to represent the core generative skills targeted by the infrastructure.\n- **Statistical Significance:** Improvements in downstream metrics (e.g., editing precision, entity retrieval accuracy) are measured. A dependency is established if pretraining yields a statistically significant gain of >5% (p < 0.05, paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction). Effect sizes and confidence intervals are reported to ensure robustness, and the threshold is validated across multiple tasks and datasets to avoid spurious dependencies.\n\n**Dynamic Curriculum Scheduling Mechanism:**\n\n- The model is first exposed to data for foundational capabilities.\n- When capability milestones (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) are reached on held-out validation sets, the scheduler introduces data for more complex capabilities.\n- If evaluation reveals stagnation in a capability (e.g., FID or CLIP score does not improve for 3 consecutive validation checkpoints), the scheduler increases the sampling rate of relevant data by 20% until progress resumes.\n- Task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution are progressively adjusted according to predefined thresholds and model performance.\n\n**Data Curation and Quality Assurance**\n\nData curation involves:\n\n- Automated filtering for image quality (BRISQUE < 30, aesthetic scores > 6.0), with thresholds based on prior work demonstrating strong correlation with human judgments of image quality (Mittal et al., 2012; Shu et al., 2021).\n- Deduplication (perceptual hashing with Hamming distance < 10) to ensure dataset diversity, following best practices from LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022).\n- Content safety (NSFW and watermark detectors with >95% confidence), referencing approaches from OpenAI\u2019s CLIP and LAION datasets.\n- Only images passing these filters are retained.\n\nRecaptioning and entity linking are performed by caption experts, with human review for samples that fail automated confidence thresholds (<0.85). Strict quality and diversity criteria include minimum caption length (\u22658 tokens), vocabulary coverage (at least 90% of a 10,000-word reference lexicon), and entity diversity (minimum 500 unique entities per 100,000 images), ensuring broad representation across concepts.\n\n**Relation to Prior Work**\n\nOur approach builds on and extends prior work in data curation and curriculum learning for generative models. For example, LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and Conceptual Captions (Sharma et al., 2018) focus on large-scale, high-quality text-image pairs, but do not address cross-task harmonization or capability dependencies. Curriculum GANs (Karras et al., 2018) and staged training in DALL-E 2 (Ramesh et al., 2022) introduce curriculum learning for generative models, but use fixed-stage training and do not empirically model dependencies between generative skills. In contrast, our infrastructure:\n\n- Empirically establishes and leverages dependencies between generative skills through transfer experiments, resulting in improved downstream performance (e.g., editing precision increases by 7% when preceded by T2I pretraining).\n- Ensures consistent annotation across tasks via the harmonization module, whereas previous work often uses task-specific annotation schemes, leading to inconsistencies.\n- Adapts data exposure dynamically based on real-time model progress, rather than relying on fixed-stage training.\n- Supports and is evaluated on a wider range of generative tasks (text-to-image, editing, knowledge association) within a unified framework, as demonstrated by improved performance across all CPI-Bench tasks compared to baselines.\n\n**Evaluation**\n\n*Experimental Setup:* We train 6B-parameter models on datasets of 200M images, with 10% reserved for validation. Human evaluation involves 12 raters, each scoring 500 images per model, with inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba) of 0.81. All quantitative results are averaged over 3 independent runs; 95% confidence intervals are reported.\n\n*Evaluation Metrics and Reporting:*\n- **Text-to-image:** Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID; lower is better) and CLIP score (higher is better).\n- **Editing:** Human-rated editing precision, defined as the average rater score (1\u20135 scale, where 1 = \"no visible edit,\" 5 = \"edit is precise and matches instruction exactly\").\n- **Knowledge association:** Entity retrieval accuracy (percentage of test images for which the correct entity is retrieved from a knowledge base).\n\n*Statistical Analysis:* For each metric, we use paired t-tests to compare our model to each baseline on the same set of prompts or images. The Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied to control for multiple comparisons. Preprocessing pipelines are standardized across models, and any deviations are reported.\n\n*CPI-Bench Benchmark and Baselines:* We evaluate on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark covering text-to-image generation, image editing, and knowledge association tasks. Baselines\u2014Stable Diffusion v1.5, Imagen, and DALL-E 2\u2014are chosen for their strong performance and widespread adoption, providing a rigorous comparison across all CPI-Bench tasks.\n\n*Quantitative Results:*\n- **Text-to-image:** Our 6B-parameter model achieves an FID of 6.2 \u00b1 0.2 (vs. 8.1 \u00b1 0.3 for Stable Diffusion) and a CLIP score of 0.312 \u00b1 0.004 (vs. 0.295 \u00b1 0.005).\n- **Editing precision:** Our model achieves a mean human-rated editing precision of 4.3 \u00b1 0.1 (vs. 3.8 \u00b1 0.1 for DALL-E 2).\n- **Knowledge association:** Entity retrieval accuracy on CPI-Bench is 78% \u00b1 1% (vs. 70% \u00b1 2% for Imagen).\n\nThese results demonstrate that our capability-driven data infrastructure, with explicit modeling of capability dependencies and cross-task harmonization, yields consistent improvements across all evaluated generative tasks."}}